{
  "rules": [
    "Strong negative bias across the full horizon: model levels are consistently too low versus realized prices (often by ~3–10+ EUR), especially during sustained uptrends.",
    "Trend/acceleration underreaction: when the market breaks higher, the model fails to carry momentum forward (forecasts flatten/decline while outcomes keep rising). Apply an upward drift adjustment when the last 1–2 weeks show persistent gains.",
    "Horizon-dependent bias: underprediction grows with lead time (errors tend to widen from week 1 into weeks 2–4). Use a time-increasing uplift (e.g., small in week 1, larger by weeks 3–4).",
    "Turning-point lag: the model anticipates mean reversion too early (projects pullbacks that arrive later or not at all). Delay mean-reversion effects when recent realized volatility is accompanied by higher highs/higher lows.",
    "Volatility compression: forecast path is too smooth and low-amplitude relative to realized swings. Increase response to recent realized volatility (wider forecast dispersion) rather than reverting quickly to a stable band.",
    "Post-spike behavior: after sharp rises, the model tends to fade the move (overshoots downward) while the market often consolidates at higher levels. Replace immediate fade with a 'plateau then gradual decay' heuristic."
  ]
}
